"Automated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Lear (https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf)\nciteturn32view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 773\nL0@P0: Full Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\nL1@P0: https://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=hlaq20\nL2@P0: Language Assessment Quarterly\nL3@P0: ISSN: (Print) (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/hlaq20\nL4@P0: Automated Sign Language Vocabulary\nL5@P0: Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine\nL6@P0: Ratings and Studying Learner Perceptions\nL7@P0: Franz Holzknecht, Sandrine Tornay, Alessia Battisti, Aaron Olaf Batty, Katja\nL8@P0: Tissi, Tobias Haug & Sarah Ebling\nL9@P0: To cite this article: Franz Holzknecht, Sandrine Tornay, Alessia Battisti, Aaron Olaf Batty,\nL10@P0: Katja Tissi, Tobias Haug & Sarah Ebling (26 Jun 2024): Automated Sign Language Vocabulary\nL11@P0: Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Learner Perceptions,\nL12@P0: Language Assessment Quarterly, DOI: 10.1080/15434303.2024.2364877\nL13@P0: To link to this article: https://doi.org/10.1080/15434303.2024.2364877\nL14@P0: © 2024 The Author(s). Published with\nL15@P0: license by Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.\nL16@P0: Published online: 26 Jun 2024.\nL17@P0: Submit your article to this journal\nL18@P0: View related articles\nL19@P0-1: View Crossmark dataARTICLE\nL20@P1: Automated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing\nL21@P1: Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Learner Perceptions\nL22@P1: Franz Holzknecht a, Sandrine Tornay b, Alessia Battisti c, Aaron Olaf Batty d,\nL23@P1: Katja Tissi a\nL24@P1: , Tobias Haug a, and Sarah Ebling c\nL25@P1: a\nL26@P1: University of Teacher Education in Special Needs (HfH), Zürich, Switzerland; bIdiap Research Institute, Martigny,\nL27@P1: Switzerland; c\nL28@P1: University of Zürich, Zürich, Switzerland; dKeio University, Fujisawa, Japan\nL29@P1: ABSTRACT\nL30@P1: Although automated spoken language assessment is rapidly growing,\nL31@P1: such systems have not been widely developed for signed languages.\nL32@P1: This study provides validity evidence for an automated web applica\u0002tion that was developed to assess and give feedback on handshape\nL33@P1: and hand movement of L2 learners’ Swiss German Sign Language\nL34@P1: signs. The study shows good machine-internal and human-machine\nL35@P1: agreement through many-facet Rasch analysis. Learner perceptions\nL36@P1: examined through questionnaire responses indicate that the auto\u0002mated system occasionally generated ratings which impacted the\nL37@P1: quality of feedback at the level of individual signs for individual\nL38@P1: learners. Implications are discussed from a learning-oriented assess\u0002ment perspective.\nL39@P1: INTRODUCTION\nL40@P1: For spoken languages, automated assessment systems have become increasingly common in\nL41@P1: recent years, for testing both writing and speaking. The main advantage of automated\nL42@P1: scoring and feedback systems is their cost-efficiency, as large volumes of written and spoken\nL43@P1: performances can be evaluated in a short amount of time (Van Moere & Downey, 2016).\nL44@P1: Another advantage of automated scores over human scores is that machines can more\nL45@P1: reliably rate specific mechanical aspects of language use, such as word frequency, punctua\u0002tion, or grammatical errors (Davis & Papageorgiou, 2021).\nL46@P1: For sign languages, however, automated assessment systems for language produc\u0002tion have so far not been developed on a wider scale, despite increased levels of\nL47@P1: interest by practitioners in this issue (Haug et al., 2023). This has mainly to do with\nL48@P1: the complexity of collecting and annotating large numbers of signed learner perfor\u0002mances, which is a crucial prerequisite as hundreds of rated and annotated perfor\u0002mances are usually required to develop an automated scoring system for a specific\nL49@P1: task (Van Moere & Downey, 2016). While many learner corpora for writing and\nL50@P1: speaking exist for spoken languages and new task-specific corpora can be created\nL51@P1: relatively easily (at least for written language), the development of corpora for sign\nL52@P1: languages is more complex (Fenlon & Hochgesang, 2022). This is because sign\nL53@P1: CONTACT Franz Holzknecht franz.holzknecht@hfh.ch University of Teacher Education in Special Needs (HfH),\nL54@P1: Institute for Language and Communication, Schaffhauserstrasse 239, Postfach 5850, CH-8050 Zürich, Switzerland\nL55@P1: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY\nL56@P1: https://doi.org/10.1080/15434303.2024.2364877\nL57@P1: © 2024 The Author(s). Published with license by Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.\nL58@P1: This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/\nL59@P1: by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. The terms\nL60@P1-2: on which this article has been published allow the posting of the Accepted Manuscript in a repository by the author(s) or with their consent.languages have no written form and repositories of sign language performances do\nL61@P2: not exist to the same extent as for written or spoken texts in spoken languages. As\nL62@P2: sign languages are processed visually and contain both manual and non-manual\nL63@P2: components, sign language performances first need to be video-recorded before they\nL64@P2: can be rated and error-annotated by human raters (Hanke & Fenlon, 2022). In\nL65@P2: addition, video recordings need to be of high enough quality, ideally involving 3-D\nL66@P2: cameras positioned at multiple angles, to allow for subsequent automated sign\nL67@P2: language recognition (Ebling et al., 2018). Although signed corpora are predicted\nL68@P2: to grow in the coming years (Schembri & Cormier, 2022), these practical constraints\nL69@P2: and associated costs also mean that sign language corpora are generally much\nL70@P2: smaller than corpora of spoken languages.\nL71@P2: Due to these challenges, most existing automated assessment and feedback systems for\nL72@P2: sign language production are not based on adataset of signed language performances, but\nL73@P2: on direct comparisons of a learner’s video with an L1 signer’s reference video. Several such\nL74@P2: systems have been developed over the last years. For example, Ellis et al. (2016) and Phan et\nL75@P2: al. (2018) developed a feedback system for Auslan (Australian Sign Language) that rates\nL76@P2: individual signs recorded through a Microsoft Kinect camera as either correct or incorrect\nL77@P2: by comparing them to a reference video. The system also provides visual feedback on\nL78@P2: incorrectly performed signs using avatars. Another system that requires users to be\nL79@P2: recorded through a Microsoft Kinect camera was developed by Chai et al. (2017). In this\nL80@P2: application, learners of Chinese Sign Language are assessed on their production of indivi\u0002dual signs. They receive an overall score and separate scores for hand movement and\nL81@P2: handshape, in relation to a reference video. Finally, Liu et al. (2024) describe the develop\u0002ment of a sign language recognition and feedback system which only requires a standard\nL82@P2: camera. Users first learn how to produce a sign and are then evaluated on their production,\nL83@P2: including feedback on hand position and handshape.\nL84@P2: These developments are promising, but certain limitations remain. First and fore\u0002most, as outlined above, these systems are not based on a dataset of signed learner\nL85@P2: performances, but rather on a direct comparison of the learner’s production video with\nL86@P2: one or several reference videos by a single L1 signer. The systems therefore do not\nL87@P2: account for natural variation between different signers (see also Bayley et al., 2015).\nL88@P2: For this reason, it is important that sign language recognition and assessment algo\u0002rithms are based on a representative dataset including both multiple L1 signers and L2\nL89@P2: learners, as is the case for automated assessment systems of writing and speaking in\nL90@P2: spoken languages. Another limitation of some current applications concerns the\nL91@P2: camera required to record learner performances. The systems which are based on\nL92@P2: Microsoft Kinect cameras (Chai et al., 2017; Ellis et al., 2016; Phan et al., 2018) need to\nL93@P2: be updated to other hardware in the future, as the Microsoft Kinect has since been\nL94@P2: discontinued. More user-friendly solutions requiring less sophisticated camera tech\u0002nology for recording sign language performances, e.g. standard cameras as used by Liu\nL95@P2: et al. (2017), are necessary to implement automated sign language assessment systems\nL96@P2: on a larger scale. It should also be noted that all existing systems, including the one\nL97@P2: described in the current paper, so far only rate the performance on manual compo\u0002nents of individual signs. The automated recognition and assessment of continuous\nL98@P2: signing and of non-manual components such as mouthing, eye-brow movements, head\nL99@P2: movements, or eye-gaze, which are important parts of sign language communication,\nL100@P2-3: 2 F. HOLZKNECHT ET AL.is an ongoing research problem, which an interdisciplinary research project is cur\u0002rently working on (see Haug & Holzknecht, 2020).\nL101@P3: Apart from these technical and logistical challenges, another aspect which has so far not\nL102@P3: been addressed sufficiently in the field of automated sign language assessment is the\nL103@P3: comparison of the systems with human raters. In spoken languages, it is standard to\nL104@P3: compare automated ratings with a large-enough number of human expert ratings to\nL105@P3: statistically analyze their agreement and the extent to which they measure the same under\u0002lying trait (Van Moere & Downey, 2016). Although limited studies involving only one\nL106@P3: human rater have been conducted for some of the systems presented above (Chai et al.,\nL107@P3: 2017; Liu et al., 2024), comprehensive studies are still lacking.\nL108@P3: In addition, as the main objective of all existing automated sign language assessments is\nL109@P3: to promote and foster learning, they should also be studied from a learning-oriented\nL110@P3: assessment (LOA) perspective. LOA has traditionally been under-represented in the assess\u0002ment literature, and only in the last decade have researchers begun to more systematically\nL111@P3: focus on the perspectives and insights of the learners themselves (Jin, 2023). Carless and\nL112@P3: colleagues propose three key components that are central to LOA approaches:\nL113@P3: 1. Designing LOA tasks. The tasks learners need to complete should be authentic and elicit\nL114@P3: competencies that mirror those needed in real-life.\nL115@P3: 2. Developing learners’ evaluative expertise. Learners should develop skills that enable them to\nL116@P3: critically analyze their work and the work of their peers with a view to support learning.\nL117@P3: 3. Learners’ engagement with feedback. Learners need to understand and actively engage with\nL118@P3: the feedback provided to them so they can use it appropriately to improve learning\nL119@P3: outcomes.\nL120@P3: (Carless, 2015; Carless et al., 2006, pp.964–965).\nL121@P3: Automated sign language assessment and feedback systems should ideally also encapsulate\nL122@P3: these three components. However, it has not been studied whether and to what extent these\nL123@P3: principles are met in existing systems.\nL124@P3: The current paper addresses some of these challenges. Our aim was to validate an\nL125@P3: automated sign language assessment and feedback system for Swiss German Sign\nL126@P3: Language (Deutschschweizerische Gebärdensprache, DSGS), which is based on a large\nL127@P3: dataset of signed L1 and L2 productions, through (1) comparisons of automated ratings\nL128@P3: with those of human expert raters, and (2) learner feedback questionnaire data on the\nL129@P3: usefulness of the system with regards to improved learning outcomes. We first briefly\nL130@P3: describe the system in the next section before outlining the study in detail.\nL131@P3: THE WEB SMILE DEMO\nL132@P3: The Web SMILE Demo is an application designed for learning and automatically assessing\nL133@P3: the production of individual lexemes in sign languages. The current version of the Demo\nL134@P3: was developed in the course of the SMILE (Scalable Multimodal Sign Language Technology\nL135@P3: for Sign Language Learning and Assessment) research projects on DSGS (Ebling et al., 2018;\nL136@P3: Haug & Holzknecht, 2020). Like the systems outlined above, the Web SMILE Demo\nL137@P3: currently only provides scores and feedback on manual components of individual lexemes\nL138@P3: and does not (yet) consider non-manuals. However, in contrast to other existing systems, it\nL139@P3-4: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 3is based on a dataset of performances consisting of multiple productions of 100 DSGS\nL140@P4: lexemes by 9 L1 DSGS signers and 19 L2 DSGS learners, which have been annotated using\nL141@P4: six category/acceptability criteria at lexeme-level: “(1) same lexeme as target sign (same\nL142@P4: meaning, same form), (2) same lexeme as target sign (same meaning, slightly different\nL143@P4: form), (3) same lexeme as target sign (same meaning, different form), (4) same lexeme as\nL144@P4: target sign (slightly different meaning, slightly different form), (5) different lexeme than\nL145@P4: target sign (same meaning, different form), (6) different lexeme than target sign (different\nL146@P4: meaning, different form)” (see Ebling et al., 2018). In addition, the Demo does not require\nL147@P4: special external camera equipment or specific server capacities but can be run online on any\nL148@P4: modern computer with an integrated or connected webcam. The Demo currently includes\nL149@P4: 16 DSGS lexemes, but the number of lexemes will be extended to 100 during the next\nL150@P4: project (for details about the 16 lexemes, including pictures and links to videos, please see\nL151@P4: Appendix 1). Although it is only available for DSGS, the Demo’s underlying algorithms can\nL152@P4: be run on data from any sign language. We used the first version of the Demo for the\nL153@P4: current study (see Tornay et al., 2020).\nL154@P4: To practice or be assessed on the production of lexemes with the Web SMILE Demo,\nL155@P4: learners need a personal computer with a webcam and internet access. After logging in via a\nL156@P4: web browser, learners first need to sign a data protection consent form. They are then\nL157@P4: directed to a“How it works” section, which describes the different processes and subpages\nL158@P4: and includes contact details in case of technical or other problems.\nL159@P4: Once they have familiarised themselves with the instructions in the “How it works”\nL160@P4: section, learners first choose which lexeme they would like to practice. Learners are\nL161@P4: prompted to produce each lexeme by means of the German language equivalent in written\nL162@P4: form. Before recording their performance, they can watch a reference video of the target\nL163@P4: lexeme performed by an L1 user of DSGS (this option can be deactivated for assessment\nL164@P4: purposes). The current capability of the Web SMILE Demo is thus consistent with the\nL165@P4: discrete, context-independent approach of conventional form-recall vocabulary tests. After\nL166@P4: recording their performance, the learner’s video is sent to a server for scoring. Processing of\nL167@P4: feedback and scores currently takes about 10 to 12 seconds (depending on the internet\nL168@P4: speed), but the aim is to further reduce processing time during the next phase of the project.\nL169@P4: After processing, the Demo provides automated feedback on five different aspects of the\nL170@P4: learner’s performance: handshape is assessed as either correct or incorrect for both domi\u0002nant and non-dominant hand (signers usually have a dominant hand which they use to\nL171@P4: produce one-handed signs, see Watkins & Thompson, 2017), hand movement is rated on a\nL172@P4: scale for both hands, and overall score is rated according to the same scale as hand\nL173@P4: Table 1. Rating Scale for Hand Movement and Overall\nL174@P4: Score.\nL175@P4: Score Description\nL176@P4: 4 little or no divergence from the target sign\nL177@P4: 3 small divergence from the target sign\nL178@P4: 2 significant divergence from the target sign\nL179@P4: 1 large divergence from the target sign\nL180@P4: 0* the sign does not correspond to the target sign\nL181@P4: *This is only reported for overall score. The degrees of divergence are\nL182@P4: determined through algorithms based on the category/acceptability\nL183@P4: annotations of the underlying dataset (see Ebling et al., 2018).\nL184@P4-5: 4 F. HOLZKNECHT ET AL.movement. For the overall score, the algorithm uses the handshape and hand movement\nL185@P5: features of both hands to compute the alignment between the learner’s production and the\nL186@P5: model. The rating scale for hand movement and overall score is shown in Table 1. Other\nL187@P5: manual components such as palm orientation or location are not yet assessed (these\nL188@P5: parameters have since been implemented or are being implemented but were not yet fully\nL189@P5: developed at the time of data collection).\nL190@P5: In addition to the scores, feedback is provided visually using the reference-video’s\nL191@P5: skeleton overlaid with the learner-video’s skeleton, whereby incorrect handshapes are\nL192@P5: marked by a red circle and hand movements by a red line in the feedback video. This\nL193@P5: enables learners to see at which point during the production and for which aspect an error\nL194@P5: occurred and for how long it lasted. It can be argued that this type of feedback is in line with\nL195@P5: the LOA principles outlined above as learners need to engage with the information provided\nL196@P5: and thereby develop their evaluative expertise.\nL197@P5: The features of the Web SMILE Demo can be modified to different domains of use. First\nL198@P5: and foremost, the Demo is designed to be a learning tool in that it should help sign language\nL199@P5: learners perform the manual a spects of individual signs correctly. However, by deactivating\nL200@P5: access to the reference video, the Demo can also be used as an assessment tool, for example\nL201@P5: to test learners’ vocabulary knowledge at the end of acourse.\nL202@P5: RESEARCH QUESTIONS\nL203@P5: The aims of the current paper were to 1) study the extent to which automated scores from\nL204@P5: the Web SMILE Demo correspond to human raters’ scores, and 2) gauge learners’ feedback\nL205@P5: regarding their experience with the Demo and the automated scores they receive. The\nL206@P5: following research questions were investigated:\nL207@P5: (1) To what extent do human expert ratings agree with automated ratings for the five\nL208@P5: aspects rated by the Web SMILE Demo (handshape and hand movement for both\nL209@P5: dominant and non-dominant hand and overall score)?\nL210@P5: (2) To what extent do learners perceive the automated scores and the automated feed\u0002back generated through the Web SMILE Demo to be useful?\nL211@P5: METHODS\nL212@P5: Three types of data were collected to answer the research questions: (1) automated scores\nL213@P5: from the Web SMILE Demo based on learner performances, (2) human raters’ scores based\nL214@P5: on the same learner performances, and (3) learner feedback questionnaire responses. In\nL215@P5: addition, all participants filled in a background questionnaire. The participants and data\nL216@P5: collection procedures are described below.\nL217@P5: PARTICIPANTS\nL218@P5: Sign language learners\nL219@P5: As a first step, we recruited 16 participants to work with the Demo and receive\nL220@P5: automated scores. The participants were all sign language interpreting learners (DSGS\nL221@P5-6: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 5and German) in their first year of a bachelor’s interpreting degree at a University in\nL222@P6: Switzerland. They received study credit points for their participation in this project. All\nL223@P6: learners were female, between 22 and 46 years old (M = 34.25, SD = 8.43), and either\nL224@P6: from Switzerland (N = 13), Liechtenstein (N = 2), or Germany (N = 1). In terms of their\nL225@P6: previous education, six learners had completed a professional certification (an appren\u0002ticeship or other professional training), eight learners had completed a degree in tertiary\nL226@P6: education (at a university or college), and two learners had completed both. German\nL227@P6: was the first language of all except one learner, whose first language was Romansh (a\nL228@P6: Romance language spoken in some eastern parts of Switzerland), however this learner\nL229@P6: indicated that she mostly used German in her daily life. None of the learners had\nL230@P6: hearing impairments, but three learners had a deaf partner or a deaf person in their\nL231@P6: extended family.\nL232@P6: As part of the entry requirements for the interpreting degree, the learners needed to\nL233@P6: provide a DSGS course certificate, and we asked them which course they had completed.\nL234@P6: Ten learners had completed a CEFR (Common European Framework of Reference for\nL235@P6: Languages) A1-level course and 4 learners a CEFR A2-level course. We also asked the learners\nL236@P6: to self-rate their DSGS proficiency using the Language Experience and Proficiency\nL237@P6: Questionnaire (LEAP-Q, see Marian et al., 2007). Learners had to indicate their receptive\nL238@P6: and productive DSGS proficiency separately on an 11-point scale (where 0 = no proficiency\nL239@P6: and 10 = perfect proficiency). Learners rated themselves between 2 and 7 for reception (M =\nL240@P6: 3.25, SD = 1.44) and between 1 and 6 for production (M = 3.06, SD = 1.57).\nL241@P6: Sign language raters\nL242@P6: In addition to the sign language learners, we also recruited 14 expert raters for the study. The\nL243@P6: raters were all training to be DSGS teachers of deaf or hearing-impaired children and they\nL244@P6: were in the first year of a teacher training course at a University in Switzerland. The raters\nL245@P6: were compensated monetarily for their participation in the study. Ten raters were female and\nL246@P6: four were male. They were between 21 and 59 years old (M = 39.21, SD = 10.85), and either\nL247@P6: from Switzerland (N = 12), Brazil (N = 1), or Eritrea (N = 1); the two raters from Brazil and\nL248@P6: Eritrea had lived in Switzerland for 9 years. Prior to their teaching degree, 11 raters had\nL249@P6: completed a professional certification (an apprenticeship or other professional training), 1\nL250@P6: rater had completed a degree in tertiary education (at a university or college), and 2 raters had\nL251@P6: completed both. All raters were deaf (N = 12) or severely hearing impaired (N = 2).\nL252@P6: The raters had different DSGS backgrounds. Five raters acquired DSGS from birth as an\nL253@P6: L1, while the remaining nine raters picked up DSGS later in life and had learned it for 9 to\nL254@P6: 32 years (M = 21.13, SD = 7.51, with one missing response). One rater had learned Eritrean\nL255@P6: Sign Language as an L1 before acquiring DSGS, while 8 raters first acquired German (N = 7)\nL256@P6: or Albanian (N=1) as an L1. This is a common phenomenon among deaf people, who are\nL257@P6: often first exposed to a spoken language before acquiring a sign language (see Humphries et\nL258@P6: al., 2016). On the LEAP-Q, ten raters indicated that DSGS was the language they were most\nL259@P6: proficient in out of all the languages they knew, two raters put DSGS in second place, one\nL260@P6: rater in third, and one rater in fourth. This last rater, however, used two of the languages\nL261@P6: they were more proficient in only for writing. The raters self-rated their DSGS proficiency\nL262@P6: between 7 and 10 for reception (M = 8.93, SD = 1.00) and between 6 and 10 for production\nL263@P6: (M = 8.21, SD = 1.37), so they were all proficient to highly proficient users of DSGS.\nL264@P6-7: 6 F. HOLZKNECHT ET AL.DATA COLLECTION PROCEDURE\nL265@P7: Learner performances\nL266@P7: The first part of the data collection involved the learners performing the 16 lexemes through\nL267@P7: the Web SMILE Demo from home via their personal computers. In preparation for this, the\nL268@P7: learners first each booked a one-hour timeslot so we could monitor the Demo performance\nL269@P7: remotely for each learner and assist them in case of technical or other issues.\nL270@P7: One week before their timeslot the learners received email instructions, which included\nL271@P7: software requirements (the Demo currently only runs on Google Chrome, so learners were\nL272@P7: asked to install the browser prior to data collection) and room setup (they were asked to be\nL273@P7: in a quiet room with a neutral background and stable internet connection). The learners\nL274@P7: also received access to the “How it works” section of the Demo and they were asked to\nL275@P7: familiarize themselves with it before their booked timeslot, to save time on the day of data\nL276@P7: collection. In addition, in the email we told the learners that they would have to produce\nL277@P7: each lexeme three times: the first time without looking at the reference video, the second\nL278@P7: time with looking at the reference video and to the best of their ability, and the third time\nL279@P7: with a deliberate mistake in their production such as incorrect handshape or hand move\u0002ment. This enabled us to systematically investigate the extent to which the Web SMILE\nL280@P7: Demo detected errors as compared to the human raters. Feedback was provided to the\nL281@P7: learners after each recording.\nL282@P7: The learners received another email one hour before their booked time slot, which\nL283@P7: included login information (username and password), a phone number and email address\nL284@P7: to contact us in case they needed assistance, and the link to the learner feedback ques\u0002tionnaire. During the data collection, the learners could choose in which order they wanted\nL285@P7: to produce the lexemes.\nL286@P7: Learner feedback questionnaire\nL287@P7: After the learners completed the Demo (i.e., after they produced each of the 16 lexemes\nL288@P7: three times) they filled out a feedback questionnaire via LimeSurvey. The questionnaire was\nL289@P7: piloted with three participants. After the piloting phase, we slightly changed the wording of\nL290@P7: three questions. The final version of the questionnaire included four main sections and a\nL291@P7: total of 22 questions and took about 10 minutes to complete. Sixteen questions were\nL292@P7: formulated as statements to which the learners had to indicate their level agreement on a\nL293@P7: Likert-scale (from “1 = fully agree” to “6 = fully disagree”), the remaining six questions were\nL294@P7: open-ended. In the first section, learners were asked seven Likert-scale and two open-ended\nL295@P7: questions about their experience with the Demo, such as clarity of instructions and\nL296@P7: technical difficulties. The second section included two Likert-scale questions and one\nL297@P7: open-ended question about their experienced difficulties in producing the lexemes. In the\nL298@P7: third section, we asked learners in six Likert-scale questions how relevant the skills practiced\nL299@P7: through the Demo were for their learning of DSGS, how interesting they found the Demo,\nL300@P7: and whether they enjoyed working on the tasks. Finally, the last section included one Likert\u0002scale question and three open-ended questions about the quality of feedback the learners\nL301@P7: received through the Demo and whether they had any suggestions on how to improve the\nL302@P7: system. All learners answered the questions in the same order. The questionnaire was\nL303@P7: administered in German and is also available in English at http://tinyurl.com/2d8ksvah.\nL304@P7-8: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 7Rater training and rating form\nL305@P8: After recruiting the 14 raters, we first trained them during two three-hour workshops\nL306@P8: as part of their teacher training at the university. For each performance, the raters\nL307@P8: scored the five different aspects according to the same scheme as used in the Demo:\nL308@P8: handshape individually for both hands (as either correct or incorrect), hand movement\nL309@P8: individually for both hands (on a scale from 1 to 4), and overall score (on a scale from\nL310@P8: 0 to 4, see Table 1 above). In the first workshop, we introduced the rating procedure\nL311@P8: and rating scale and asked them to familiarise themselves with it. Following this\nL312@P8: introduction, the raters rated an example performance on all five aspects during the\nL313@P8: workshop, and the results were compared and discussed. For the overall score, the\nL314@P8: raters were trained to base the ratings on their overall impression of the handshape\nL315@P8: and hand movement of both hands, as conveyed in the rating scale descriptors. After\nL316@P8: the initial training workshop, the raters received an Excel rating form including video\nL317@P8: links to three more example performances and they were asked to rate these on all\nL318@P8: aspects individually at home. During the next workshop one week later, the ratings\nL319@P8: were again compared and discussed as a group, to ensure that there was a mutual\nL320@P8: understanding of the scale descriptors and the procedure. In the course of the work\u0002shops, we also refined the rating instructions and changed the Excel rating form\nL321@P8: slightly to make them clearer.\nL322@P8: After training, we sent the individual Excel rating forms including detailed\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn32view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31view0\",\"pattern\":\"quatro\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.scielo.br/j/tla/a/5CmYmQZYRG3GMHF9Ymq8c7x/?lang=pt: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn32view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31view0\",\"pattern\":\"licença\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.scielo.br/j/tla/a/5CmYmQZYRG3GMHF9Ymq8c7x/?lang=pt: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn32view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31view0\",\"pattern\":\"incerto\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.scielo.br/j/tla/a/5CmYmQZYRG3GMHF9Ymq8c7x/?lang=pt: (400) Timeout fetching"
